---
// biome-ignore-all lint/correctness/noUnusedImports: Astro resolves frontmatter bindings in the template.
import DocCallout from "@/components/marketing/DocCallout.astro";
import DocsShell from "@/components/marketing/DocsShell.astro";
import MarketingLayout from "@/layouts/MarketingLayout.astro";
import { publicSiteLinks } from "@/lib/public-site";
---

<MarketingLayout
	kind="article"
	title="Nearzero Agent — it can see your infra. Production writes start off."
	description="The Agent can read deploys, logs, domains, and servers. It can only change what your role and workspace policy allow. Production writes stay off until an administrator turns that gate on."
>
	<DocsShell
		title="The Agent can see your infra. It cannot change production until you turn that on."
		summary="It reads deploys, logs, domains, and servers through scoped tools. Mutations need your role, administrator policy, and a production gate that starts off."
		activePath={publicSiteLinks.agentSafety}
		toc={[
			{ href: "#model", label: "Operating model" },
			{ href: "#permissions", label: "Permissions" },
			{ href: "#secrets", label: "Secret handling" },
			{ href: "#builds", label: "Build boundaries" },
			{ href: "#checklist", label: "Administrator checklist" },
		]}
		previous={{ href: publicSiteLinks.domainsAndHttps, label: "Domains and HTTPS" }}
		next={{ href: publicSiteLinks.docs, label: "Documentation home" }}
	>
		<section id="model" class="nz-doc-section">
			<span class="nz-doc-section__index">01</span>
			<h2>Observe, then act, then verify</h2>
			<p>
				The Agent can read projects, environments, services, deploys, logs,
				domains, servers, and Git metadata. It calls structured Nearzero tools
				and uses the returned result for the next step.
			</p>
			<figure class="nz-doc-flow">
				<figcaption class="nz-visually-hidden">The Agent observes context, acts through a scoped tool, and verifies the returned result.</figcaption>
				<div><small>Observe</small><strong>Read context</strong><span>State, logs, policy</span></div>
				<i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
				<div><small>Act</small><strong>Call a scoped tool</strong><span>Only when authorized</span></div>
				<i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
				<div><small>Verify</small><strong>Use the result</strong><span>Never invent success</span></div>
			</figure>
			<DocCallout title="This is not unrestricted autonomy" tone="note">
				<p>The Agent is a deployment copilot. It does not run in the background, inspect arbitrary source, or act outside the tools and organization Nearzero provides.</p>
			</DocCallout>
		</section>

		<section id="permissions" class="nz-doc-section">
			<span class="nz-doc-section__index">02</span>
			<h2>The server enforces policy, not the model</h2>
			<p>
				Role checks and Agent action policies run in Nearzero. Production writes
				start disabled. An administrator has to turn that gate on.
			</p>
			<div class="nz-doc-table-wrap">
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Boundary</th><th>Behavior</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td>Organization scope</td><td>Tools operate within the active organization and the requesting user’s access.</td></tr>
						<tr><td>Role and action policy</td><td>Each protected action is checked server-side before infrastructure work begins.</td></tr>
						<tr><td>Production environments</td><td>Agent mutations are denied by default until an administrator enables them.</td></tr>
						<tr><td>Tool results</td><td>The Agent must not claim success unless the invoked tool returns a successful result.</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</div>
			<DocCallout title="Turning on production writes is an admin decision" tone="critical">
				<p>Do it only after reviewing organization roles, server access, Git providers, connected model credentials, and recovery procedures.</p>
			</DocCallout>
		</section>

		<section id="secrets" class="nz-doc-section">
			<span class="nz-doc-section__index">03</span>
			<h2>Do not paste secrets into chat</h2>
			<p>
				Do not paste API keys, tokens, passwords, private keys, or secret-bearing
				commands into Agent chat. Nearzero uses dedicated UI handoffs for service
				environment values and SSH setup so the model does not need the raw value.
			</p>
			<ul class="nz-doc-checklist">
				<li><span>1</span><div><strong>Keep browser-exposed prefixes public.</strong><p><code>NEXT_PUBLIC_</code>, <code>PUBLIC_</code>, and <code>VITE_</code> values are intended for client bundles and must never contain credentials.</p></div></li>
				<li><span>2</span><div><strong>Treat database storage as privileged.</strong><p>API redaction is not encryption at rest. Encrypt volumes and backups, and restrict Postgres access.</p></div></li>
				<li><span>3</span><div><strong>Protect SSH material.</strong><p>Dedicated remote setup accounts have root-equivalent Docker and privilege access.</p></div></li>
				<li><span>4</span><div><strong>Rotate after suspected exposure.</strong><p>Redaction limits accidental display; it cannot revoke a credential that untrusted code already received.</p></div></li>
			</ul>
		</section>

		<section id="builds" class="nz-doc-section">
			<span class="nz-doc-section__index">04</span>
			<h2>Build secrets are not a sandbox</h2>
			<p>
				Generated build phases keep configured values out of ordinary command
				arguments and use BuildKit secret mounts where supported. Durable logs are
				redacted before storage. These controls reduce accidental leakage, but
				they cannot make untrusted build code safe.
			</p>
			<DocCallout title="A repository that receives a secret can deliberately leak it" tone="warning">
				<p>A Dockerfile, dependency hook, buildpack, install command, or build command can print, transmit, or bake a supplied value into an image or browser bundle. Give build secrets only to repositories and dependencies you trust.</p>
			</DocCallout>
			<ul>
				<li>Use the dedicated build-secret field when a Dockerfile needs a credential.</li>
				<li>Keep non-sensitive build arguments separate from private runtime values.</li>
				<li>Pin user-authored base images and production builder images by digest.</li>
				<li>Rotate a credential after any suspicious or untrusted build.</li>
			</ul>
		</section>

		<section id="checklist" class="nz-doc-section">
			<span class="nz-doc-section__index">05</span>
			<h2>Before you enable the Agent on production</h2>
			<ul class="nz-doc-checklist">
				<li><span>✓</span><div><strong>Roles match actual responsibilities.</strong><p>Only trusted operators can mutate production or deploy privileged Compose workloads.</p></div></li>
				<li><span>✓</span><div><strong>Model access is organization-scoped.</strong><p>Use the intended BYOK provider and rotate its key through the settings flow.</p></div></li>
				<li><span>✓</span><div><strong>Production mutations remain off until needed.</strong><p>Enable them only after backups, rollback, and observability are tested.</p></div></li>
				<li><span>✓</span><div><strong>Secrets stay out of chat and public prefixes.</strong><p>Use the secure configuration surfaces and inspect client bundles before release.</p></div></li>
				<li><span>✓</span><div><strong>Every action is verified.</strong><p>Check deployment state, logs, domain resolution, and runtime health after changes.</p></div></li>
			</ul>
		</section>
	</DocsShell>
</MarketingLayout>
